::CW_U03_L04_Q01::Central idea: Avoiding responsibility can make guilt grow. Which detail best supports it?{
~A. The sky is blue.#Incorrect. This does not connect to responsibility.
=B. The character hides the broken key after blaming someone else.#Correct. Hiding evidence after blame shows guilt growing.
~C. The character eats cereal.#Incorrect. This is unrelated unless tied to guilt.
~D. The hallway is long.#Incorrect. This does not support the idea by itself.
}
::CW_U03_L04_Q02::Passage: Mina says she does not need help, then drops three boxes and finally lets her sister carry one. What central idea is best supported?{
~A. Boxes are always empty.#Incorrect. The boxes are difficult to carry.
~B. Sisters never help.#Incorrect. The sister helps.
~C. Dropping things is the only idea.#Incorrect. The drop supports a larger idea.
=D. Accepting help can be a form of strength.#Correct. Mina changes from refusal to shared effort.
}
::CW_U03_L04_Q03::Central idea: Memory can make a new place feel less lonely. Which detail is most relevant?{
=A. The narrator hangs a photo from home above the new desk.#Correct. The photo connects memory to the new place.
~B. The desk has four screws.#Incorrect. This is unrelated unless connected to memory.
~C. The window is square.#Incorrect. Shape alone does not support the idea.
~D. The floor is flat.#Incorrect. This does not develop memory or loneliness.
}
::CW_U03_L04_Q04::A draft includes a long description of an unrelated concert poster. The central idea is about avoiding an apology. What revision decision is best?{
~A. Keep it because all details are equally useful.#Incorrect. Irrelevant detail can distract.
~B. Add more unrelated posters.#Incorrect. This increases distraction.
=C. Cut the poster or revise it so it connects to the apology conflict.#Correct. Details should support the central idea.
~D. Remove the apology instead.#Incorrect. The apology is central.
}
::CW_U03_L04_Q05::Central idea: True confidence includes admitting what you do not know. Which detail supports it?{
=A. The character asks for help with the first step in front of the group.#Correct. The action shows honest confidence.
~B. The character pretends to understand and leaves.#Incorrect. That shows avoidance.
~C. The character describes a pencil.#Incorrect. Unconnected description does not support the idea.
~D. The character refuses to speak forever.#Incorrect. That does not show confidence.
}
::CW_U03_L04_Q06::What is a relevant detail?{
~A. Any detail that is long.#Incorrect. Length does not make relevance.
~B. A detail that distracts from the idea.#Incorrect. That is irrelevant.
~C. A detail with no connection.#Incorrect. Connection is required.
=D. A detail that helps develop the central idea.#Correct. Relevance depends on connection to the idea.
}
::CW_U03_L04_Q07::Passage: Jori keeps checking the empty chair after her friend moves away. Central idea: Absence can shape how a place feels. How does the detail support the idea?{
~A. Chairs cannot matter.#Incorrect. Objects can support ideas.
=B. The empty chair makes absence visible in the setting.#Correct. The chair turns missing someone into a concrete detail.
~C. The detail is only about furniture shopping.#Incorrect. The context is absence.
~D. Moving away is unrelated.#Incorrect. It explains the empty chair's meaning.
}
::CW_U03_L04_Q08::Central idea: Small acts of care can rebuild trust. Which detail best supports it?{
~A. The character ignores the friend forever.#Incorrect. That does not rebuild trust.
~B. The character changes socks.#Incorrect. This is unrelated.
=C. The character leaves a repaired notebook on the friend's desk without demanding forgiveness.#Correct. The action shows care and patience.
~D. The character lists every desk in the room.#Incorrect. Listing desks does not support trust.
}
::CW_U03_L04_Q09::Which central idea is strongest for fiction development?{
~A. The scene is about stuff.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. A person does things.#Incorrect. This gives no direction.
~C. There is a room.#Incorrect. This is only setting.
=D. Keeping secrets can protect someone briefly but damage trust over time.#Correct. This is specific and can guide details.
}
::CW_U03_L04_Q10::Central idea: Hope can survive failure. Which detail supports it?{
=A. After the failed harvest, the farmer saves seeds for spring.#Correct. Saving seeds prepares for another chance.
~B. The farmer burns every seed.#Incorrect. That works against hope.
~C. The field has dirt.#Incorrect. This is too general.
~D. The calendar has dates.#Incorrect. Not enough connection.
}
::CW_U03_L04_Q11::Passage: Ana keeps rereading the invitation but never opens the door. Central idea: Fear can keep people from accepting connection. What detail supports it?{
~A. The door is made of wood.#Incorrect. Material alone does not support the idea.
~B. The invitation has ink.#Incorrect. This detail is too general.
=C. Ana rereads the invitation but does not open the door.#Correct. The action shows desire and hesitation.
~D. Ana knows the alphabet.#Incorrect. Not relevant.
}
::CW_U03_L04_Q12::What should a writer do with an interesting detail that does not support the central idea?{
~A. Keep it no matter what.#Incorrect. Interesting can still be distracting.
=B. Revise it so it supports the idea, or cut it.#Correct. The detail needs a purpose.
~C. Add ten more unrelated details.#Incorrect. More distraction weakens focus.
~D. Delete the central idea.#Incorrect. The central idea guides the scene.
}
::CW_U03_L04_Q13::Central idea: Pride can prevent a person from learning. Which detail supports it?{
=A. The character closes the instruction book after one page and says, "I already know."#Correct. The action and dialogue reveal pride blocking learning.
~B. The character studies carefully.#Incorrect. That works against the idea.
~C. The character asks a mentor for advice.#Incorrect. That shows openness.
~D. The character admits confusion.#Incorrect. That shows humility.
}
::CW_U03_L04_Q14::Passage: The narrator carries a cracked bowl into the new apartment because it belonged to her mother. Central idea: Memory can make change bearable. How does the bowl support it?{
~A. The bowl proves nothing changes.#Incorrect. The new apartment shows change.
~B. The bowl is unrelated because it is cracked.#Incorrect. The crack may make it more meaningful.
~C. The bowl only shows cooking instructions.#Incorrect. No recipe is mentioned.
=D. The bowl carries memory into the new place.#Correct. It connects past relationship to present change.
}
::CW_U03_L04_Q15::Which detail distracts from the central idea "honesty can repair harm"?{
~A. The character admits the lie.#Incorrect. This supports the idea.
~B. The character returns the hidden letter.#Incorrect. This supports repair.
=C. A full paragraph explains an unrelated video game level.#Correct. It does not connect to honesty or repair.
~D. The character apologizes directly.#Incorrect. This supports the idea.
}
::CW_U03_L04_Q16::Central idea: Community support makes hardship easier to face. Which detail best supports it?{
~A. Everyone closes their doors.#Incorrect. That works against community support.
=B. Neighbors take turns bringing meals after the fire.#Correct. Shared care helps the family face hardship.
~C. The house has windows.#Incorrect. Too general.
~D. A clock ticks.#Incorrect. Not enough connection.
}
::CW_U03_L04_Q17::What does "development" mean in this lesson?{
~A. The number of pages only.#Incorrect. Length is not development.
~B. A random list of objects.#Incorrect. Random lists do not develop ideas.
~C. A title with no scene.#Incorrect. Development needs content.
=D. The way an idea grows through story details and choices.#Correct. Development is how the idea is built.
}
::CW_U03_L04_Q18::Central idea: Forgiveness can begin before trust is fully rebuilt. Which detail supports it?{
=A. The character says, "I forgive you," but keeps the conversation short.#Correct. Forgiveness starts while full comfort has not returned.
~B. The character forgets the conflict entirely.#Incorrect. That removes the complexity.
~C. The character refuses any repair forever.#Incorrect. That does not show forgiveness beginning.
~D. The character buys shoes.#Incorrect. Unrelated.
}
::CW_U03_L04_Q19::Passage: Kai wants to be seen as brave, so he hides his shaking hands in his pockets before stepping forward. Central idea: Courage can exist beside fear. What detail supports it?{
~A. Kai has pockets.#Incorrect. Pockets matter only because they hide fear.
=B. Kai steps forward while hiding shaking hands.#Correct. The action shows fear and courage together.
~C. Kai never moves.#Incorrect. He steps forward.
~D. Kai feels no fear.#Incorrect. His hands shake.
}
::CW_U03_L04_Q20::Which revision best supports "kindness can interrupt cruelty"?{
~A. Add a character who changes the subject while classmates laugh.#Incorrect. This may avoid cruelty but does not clearly interrupt it.
~B. Add a weather report.#Incorrect. Weather does not support the idea by itself.
=C. Add a character who says, "Stop," and sits beside the student being mocked.#Correct. The action directly interrupts cruelty.
~D. Remove the harmed student.#Incorrect. That weakens the conflict.
}
::CW_U03_L04_Q21::Central idea: Letting go can be an act of care. Which detail supports it?{
=A. The character gives the injured bird space after opening the cage door.#Correct. Letting the bird go shows care.
~B. The character locks the cage tighter.#Incorrect. That works against letting go.
~C. The character paints a wall.#Incorrect. Unrelated.
~D. The character ignores the bird's injury.#Incorrect. That does not show care.
}
::CW_U03_L04_Q22::Which explanation is strongest? Central idea: Secrets can damage trust. Detail: The character deletes a message before her friend sees it.{
~A. It supports the idea because it is a sentence.#Incorrect. Being a sentence is not enough.
~B. It supports the idea because phones exist.#Incorrect. The phone is not the main connection.
=C. The deleted message supports the idea because hiding communication creates secrecy between friends.#Correct. It explains how evidence connects to trust.
~D. It does not matter what details do.#Incorrect. Details must connect to the central idea.
}
::CW_U03_L04_Q23::Central idea: Persistence means trying again with a wiser plan. Which detail best supports it?{
~A. The character repeats the same mistake without thinking.#Incorrect. That lacks a wiser plan.
~B. The character quits immediately.#Incorrect. That does not show persistence.
~C. The character names the river only.#Incorrect. Naming alone is not development.
=D. After the bridge collapses, the character studies the river before rebuilding.#Correct. Trying again with study shows wiser persistence.
}
::CW_U03_L04_Q24::What is the best use of central idea during drafting?{
~A. Ignore it after writing one sentence.#Incorrect. It should guide the scene.
=B. Use it to choose details, conflicts, and character actions that support the same meaning.#Correct. Central idea guides development.
~C. Use it only for the title.#Incorrect. It affects the whole scene.
~D. Replace all details with one vague word.#Incorrect. Fiction needs concrete support.
}
::CW_U03_L04_Q25::A student writes, "This detail supports the idea because I like it." What feedback is best?{
=A. Explain how the detail develops the central idea, not just personal preference.#Correct. Support requires a clear connection to meaning.
~B. Personal preference is always enough evidence.#Incorrect. The detail must support the idea.
~C. Remove the central idea from the answer.#Incorrect. The idea is necessary.
~D. Add unrelated details until it feels longer.#Incorrect. Length does not create support.
}
